This is a recursive version. It tries every simple path from the source and keeps the shortest length found so far. A cell is marked while it is on the current path, then unmarked when the recursion returns, so another path can use it later.

The pruning check skips paths that are already no better than the current answer. This is easy to trace on small grids, but BFS is usually a better choice for a large maze.

Dry run: from `(0, 0)`, the recursion marks that cell, tries each open neighbor, and adds one to the step count. When it reaches the destination, `best` is updated. Backtracking then checks other possible routes.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int ShortestPath(List<List<int>> grid, int[] source, int[] destination) {
        if (grid == null || grid.Count == 0 || grid[0].Count == 0) return -1;

        int rows = grid.Count;
        int cols = grid[0].Count;
        int startRow = source[0];
        int startCol = source[1];
        int endRow = destination[0];
        int endCol = destination[1];

        if (grid[startRow][startCol] == 0 || grid[endRow][endCol] == 0) {
            return -1;
        }
        if (startRow == endRow && startCol == endCol) return 0;

        bool[,] onPath = new bool[rows, cols];
        int best = int.MaxValue;
        int[] rowChange = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] colChange = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        Search(startRow, startCol, 0);
        return best == int.MaxValue ? -1 : best;

        void Search(int row, int col, int steps) {
            if (steps >= best) return;
            if (row == endRow && col == endCol) {
                best = steps;
                return;
            }

            onPath[row, col] = true;
            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                int nextRow = row + rowChange[i];
                int nextCol = col + colChange[i];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                    nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols ||
                    grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 0 ||
                    onPath[nextRow, nextCol]) {
                    continue;
                }

                Search(nextRow, nextCol, steps + 1);
            }
            onPath[row, col] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: exponential in the worst case because many different paths may be checked.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the path marks and recursion stack.

When the source and destination are far apart, searching from both ends can meet sooner. Each side keeps a frontier for one distance layer. I expand the smaller frontier and stop when a new cell touches a cell already seen from the other side.

The grid is not changed. A `Set` stores coordinates as strings, which keeps the code short.

Important edge cases are handled first: an empty grid, a blocked endpoint, and source equal to destination.

```javascript
class Solution {
    shortestPath(grid, source, destination) {
        if (!grid.length || !grid[0].length) return -1;

        const rows = grid.length;
        const cols = grid[0].length;
        const [sr, sc] = source;
        const [dr, dc] = destination;

        if (grid[sr][sc] === 0 || grid[dr][dc] === 0) return -1;
        if (sr === dr && sc === dc) return 0;

        const startKey = `${sr},${sc}`;
        const endKey = `${dr},${dc}`;
        let startFrontier = [startKey];
        let endFrontier = [endKey];
        const seenFromStart = new Map([[startKey, 0]]);
        const seenFromEnd = new Map([[endKey, 0]]);
        let startDepth = 0;
        let endDepth = 0;
        const changes = [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]];

        while (startFrontier.length && endFrontier.length) {
            const fromStart = startFrontier.length <= endFrontier.length;
            const frontier = fromStart ? startFrontier : endFrontier;
            const ownSeen = fromStart ? seenFromStart : seenFromEnd;
            const otherSeen = fromStart ? seenFromEnd : seenFromStart;
            const nextFrontier = [];
            const depth = fromStart ? startDepth : endDepth;

            for (const key of frontier) {
                const [row, col] = key.split(',').map(Number);

                for (const [rowChange, colChange] of changes) {
                    const nextRow = row + rowChange;
                    const nextCol = col + colChange;
                    if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                        nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols ||
                        grid[nextRow][nextCol] === 0) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    const nextKey = `${nextRow},${nextCol}`;
                    if (otherSeen.has(nextKey)) {
                        return depth + 1 + otherSeen.get(nextKey);
                    }
                    if (!ownSeen.has(nextKey)) {
                        ownSeen.set(nextKey, depth + 1);
                        nextFrontier.push(nextKey);
                    }
                }
            }

            if (fromStart) {
                startFrontier = nextFrontier;
                startDepth++;
            } else {
                endFrontier = nextFrontier;
                endDepth++;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols)` in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the two visited maps and frontiers.

A normal BFS fits here because every move has the same cost. The queue visits cells in distance order, so the first time the destination is reached, that distance is the shortest one.

The grid is left unchanged. A separate distance matrix also makes it easy to avoid visiting the same cell again.

Dry run: if the source is `(0, 0)`, its distance is `0`. Its open neighbors get distance `1`, then their open neighbors get distance `2`, and so on until the destination is found.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <utility>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int shortestPath(vector<vector<int>> &grid, pair<int, int> source,
                     pair<int, int> destination) {
        int rows = grid.size();
        if (rows == 0) return -1;
        int cols = grid[0].size();
        if (cols == 0) return -1;

        int sr = source.first, sc = source.second;
        int dr = destination.first, dc = destination.second;
        if (grid[sr][sc] == 0 || grid[dr][dc] == 0) return -1;
        if (source == destination) return 0;

        vector<vector<int>> distance(rows, vector<int>(cols, -1));
        queue<pair<int, int>> cells;
        cells.push(source);
        distance[sr][sc] = 0;

        int moveRow[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int moveCol[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (!cells.empty()) {
            auto current = cells.front();
            cells.pop();
            int row = current.first;
            int col = current.second;

            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                int nextRow = row + moveRow[i];
                int nextCol = col + moveCol[i];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                    nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols) {
                    continue;
                }
                if (grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 0 ||
                    distance[nextRow][nextCol] != -1) {
                    continue;
                }

                distance[nextRow][nextCol] = distance[row][col] + 1;
                if (nextRow == dr && nextCol == dc) {
                    return distance[nextRow][nextCol];
                }
                cells.push({nextRow, nextCol});
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols)`.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the distance matrix and queue.

This uses a priority queue instead of a regular queue. The smallest known distance is always processed first, which is the usual Dijkstra pattern. It is more work than needed for a binary maze, but it still gives the correct shortest distance because each move costs one.

Blocked cells are ignored, and a destination that cannot be reached keeps its initial large distance.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    int shortestPath(int[][] grid, int[] source, int[] destination) {
        int rows = grid.length;
        if (rows == 0 || grid[0].length == 0) return -1;
        int cols = grid[0].length;

        int sr = source[0], sc = source[1];
        int dr = destination[0], dc = destination[1];
        if (grid[sr][sc] == 0 || grid[dr][dc] == 0) return -1;
        if (sr == dr && sc == dc) return 0;

        int[][] distance = new int[rows][cols];
        for (int[] row : distance) {
            Arrays.fill(row, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        }

        PriorityQueue<int[]> queue = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);
        distance[sr][sc] = 0;
        queue.offer(new int[]{0, sr, sc});

        int[] rowMove = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] colMove = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] current = queue.poll();
            int steps = current[0];
            int row = current[1];
            int col = current[2];

            if (steps != distance[row][col]) continue;
            if (row == dr && col == dc) return steps;

            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                int nextRow = row + rowMove[i];
                int nextCol = col + colMove[i];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                    nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols ||
                    grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 0) {
                    continue;
                }

                int nextSteps = steps + 1;
                if (nextSteps < distance[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                    distance[nextRow][nextCol] = nextSteps;
                    queue.offer(new int[]{nextSteps, nextRow, nextCol});
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols * log(rows * cols))`.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)`. A regular BFS would be faster here because all edges have the same cost.
